WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:04.233
A database records descriptions of its tables: column names,

00:04.437 --> 00:07.447
declared types, and rules about allowed values.

00:07.831 --> 00:09.017
We can query them.

00:09.386 --> 00:12.129
But do they tell us what a delivery measure means?

00:12.538 --> 00:15.242
Let’s inspect four invented deliveries stored

00:15.254 --> 00:18.367
in a table named deliveries, using SQLite.

00:19.413 --> 00:21.799
The column records describe three fields:

00:22.130 --> 00:25.995
a delivery identifier, a promise time, and a delivery time.

00:26.379 --> 00:28.062
All three declare TEXT.

00:28.470 --> 00:29.988
In this SQLite table,

00:30.269 --> 00:34.261
that declaration does not strictly enforce the type of every stored value.

00:34.669 --> 00:39.784
The not-null flag is one for the identifier and promise: a value is required.

00:40.167 --> 00:44.491
Zero for delivery time allows NULL, the marker for a missing value.

00:44.899 --> 00:48.177
The primary key uniquely identifies each row.

00:48.573 --> 00:51.889
Here, the delivery identifier is the sole key.

00:52.913 --> 00:56.013
Those records describe structure, or schema.

00:56.423 --> 00:58.553
They do not explain every value.

00:58.936 --> 01:01.882
A missing delivery time could mean an unfinished parcel,

01:02.163 --> 01:04.407
a late update, or an unknown time.

01:04.816 --> 01:07.979
Assume these invented records are complete: here,

01:08.247 --> 01:10.823
NULL means the parcel has not yet been delivered.

01:11.206 --> 01:14.369
Meaning can also change without changing the schema.

01:15.413 --> 01:19.457
Suppose the original promise was noon, revised to twelve thirty.

01:19.866 --> 01:22.213
A parcel arrives at twelve twenty.

01:22.621 --> 01:26.945
It is late against the original promise, but on time against the revision.

01:27.353 --> 01:29.955
We need to know which promise the column stores.

01:30.363 --> 01:32.697
Its name and type cannot settle that.

01:33.747 --> 01:35.915
Now read the complete saved query.

01:36.324 --> 01:39.513
A view is a named query, readable like a table.

01:39.921 --> 01:43.901
This SQLite view calculates its result whenever it is read.

01:44.309 --> 01:47.651
Its filter removes rows with a missing delivery time.

01:48.059 --> 01:52.829
It counts the remaining deliveries, counts those arriving by the stored promise, and

01:52.842 --> 01:54.322
calculates a percentage.

01:54.730 --> 01:58.505
The zero-denominator guard returns NULL if none are completed.

01:59.538 --> 02:02.549
Here are the four deliveries, all promised by noon.

02:02.919 --> 02:07.090
Two arrived on time, one arrived late, and one is unfinished.

02:07.499 --> 02:10.215
The filter leaves three completed deliveries.

02:10.585 --> 02:15.100
Two on time out of three completed gives 66.7%.

02:15.483 --> 02:19.680
Delivery Operations needs all four deliveries due in the denominator.

02:20.049 --> 02:22.332
Two out of four gives 50.0%.

02:22.741 --> 02:24.948
The view answers a different question.

02:25.997 --> 02:31.048
The comparison assumes every timestamp uses the same fixed UTC text format,

02:31.379 --> 02:33.165
year first, through minutes.

02:33.561 --> 02:36.776
For these values, text order matches time order.

02:37.184 --> 02:40.373
The TEXT declaration does not enforce that assumption.

02:40.742 --> 02:44.530
The saved query is metadata describing a calculation.

02:44.939 --> 02:47.745
The percentage it returns is output data.

02:48.141 --> 02:52.822
Reading the query reveals what it counts; it does not establish the business rule.

02:53.872 --> 02:56.410
Catalogues have two overlapping jobs.

02:56.820 --> 03:01.424
Database and query-engine catalogues help software find and read data.

03:01.833 --> 03:06.858
Hive Metastore describes schemas, partitions such as a day’s deliveries, storage

03:06.871 --> 03:09.052
locations, and file formats.

03:09.435 --> 03:14.167
Discovery and governance catalogues help people understand and manage data.

03:14.575 --> 03:19.129
DataHub and OpenMetadata connect technical records with definitions,

03:19.422 --> 03:21.603
responsible teams, and lineage.

03:22.011 --> 03:24.384
These are main purposes, with overlap:

03:24.703 --> 03:28.070
database catalogues can hold comments or ownership too.

03:29.080 --> 03:32.958
Inspect the structure, then check the meaning and the calculation.

03:33.367 --> 03:36.632
Ask which promise is stored, what a missing time means,

03:36.900 --> 03:39.170
and which deliveries enter the denominator.

03:39.539 --> 03:42.537
Database metadata gives useful evidence.

03:42.946 --> 03:46.504
A trustworthy measure also needs an explicit definition.
